    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Growth"
    },
    {
      "kind": "para",
      "text": "Mastercard grew revenue {c07} to ${c06}bn, the fastest in the group, and its three-year compound rate of {c27} is the only one above ten percent. Visa added {c02} to ${c01}bn, a three-year compound rate of {c26}. PayPal and Fiserv grew {c12} and {c17} respectively, with compound rates of {c28} and {c29}. Global Payments reported {c22} at ${c21}bn; that figure is on a post-divestiture basis, so the two-year compound rate of {c30} is the only growth measure comparable with the business as it now stands."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Profitability and cash generation"
    },
    {
      "kind": "para",
      "text": "The networks remain a structurally different business from the processors. Visa earned an operating margin of {c03} and a net margin of {c04}, Mastercard {c08} and {c09}. Global Payments ({c23}) and Fiserv ({c18}) sit in the twenties and PayPal is lowest at {c13}. Cash conversion is uniformly strong: every company converted more than its reported net income into free cash flow in the year, from {c15} at PayPal to {c25} at Global Payments, with Visa generating ${c31}bn and Mastercard ${c32}bn of free cash flow."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Valuation"
    },
    {
      "kind": "para",
      "text": "Our equity DCF discounts three years of free cash flow at a cost of equity between {c42} and {c44}, and assumes terminal growth of {c50}. On that basis Visa is worth ${c33}bn of equity and Mastercard ${c34}bn; per share, Mastercard ${c38}, Fiserv ${c40}, Global Payments ${c41} and PayPal ${c39}. Visa's per-share value is withheld: the company does not tag a diluted share count in its XBRL filings, and we do not estimate an input we cannot source."
    },
